==============================================================================
D15 -- CO-ADAPTATION vs SELECTION QUALITY
==============================================================================

[1] CONTROL: is `ink` selection really checkpoint-independent?
    (the premise the whole diagnostic rests on)
  [PASS] keep=0.75 ink retained_ink identical across 4 checkpoints -- values=[0.9996, 0.9996, 0.9996, 0.9996] spread=0.00e+00
  [PASS] keep=0.50 ink retained_ink identical across 4 checkpoints -- values=[0.994, 0.994, 0.994, 0.994] spread=0.00e+00
  [PASS] keep=0.35 ink retained_ink identical across 4 checkpoints -- values=[0.9502, 0.9502, 0.9502, 0.9502] spread=0.00e+00

    CONTROL (non-vacuity): the ROUTER's retained_ink must actually vary,
    otherwise there is no variation to correlate and a null is meaningless.
  [PASS] keep=0.75 router retained_ink varies across checkpoints -- range=[0.840, 0.989] spread=0.148
  [PASS] keep=0.50 router retained_ink varies across checkpoints -- range=[0.675, 0.930] spread=0.254
  [PASS] keep=0.35 router retained_ink varies across checkpoints -- range=[0.511, 0.834] spread=0.324

[2] THE HEADLINE NUMBER: how big is the decoder-only effect?
    `ink` selection is byte-identical across checkpoints (control 1),
    so the SPREAD in ink-row recall is pure decoder specialization.

    keep     ink recall range  spread(pts) mean |margin|
    0.75      75.96-77.84         1.88         1.12
    0.50      74.48-76.89         2.41         2.46
    0.35      68.06-77.60         9.54         2.15

    >> At keep=0.35 the SAME token set is read 9.54 pts differently by
       different decoders, while the router's mean claimed advantage there
       is 2.15 pts. The confound is 4.4x the effect.
  [PASS] decoder-only spread exceeds the router's mean margin at keep=0.35 -- 9.54 pts vs 2.15 pts

[3] THE SIGNATURE: does the margin grow as the router drifts FROM ink?
    H_SELECT predicts corr >= 0 (more ink kept -> better).
    H_COADAPT predicts corr <  0 (more idiosyncratic -> bigger margin).

    run      keep   router ink_ret     margin  router rec   ink rec
    run 10   0.35            0.511      +6.14       76.35     68.67
    run 10   0.50            0.675      +3.33       79.41     74.48
    run 8    0.35            0.690      +2.58       77.80     74.24
    run 8    0.50            0.790      +4.07       80.17     74.79
    run 9    0.35            0.826      -0.48       75.85     77.60
    run 11   0.35            0.834      +0.35       69.52     68.06
    run 10   0.75            0.840      +1.84       79.36     77.13
    run 9    0.50            0.922      +2.91       79.63     76.89
    run 8    0.75            0.925      -0.19       75.19     75.96
    run 11   0.50            0.930      -0.49       76.17     75.47
    run 9    0.75            0.986      +0.70       79.41     77.84
    run 11   0.75            0.989      +2.10       79.65     76.82

    POOLED n=12:  Pearson r = -0.702   Spearman rho = -0.580
  [PASS] pooled correlation is NEGATIVE (H_SELECT predicts >= 0) -- r=-0.702

    WITHIN-BUDGET (controls for the budget confound; n=4 each):
      keep=0.75  r = -0.107   (router ink_ret spread 0.148)
      keep=0.50  r = -0.614   (router ink_ret spread 0.254)
      keep=0.35  r = -0.990   (router ink_ret spread 0.324)
  [PASS] correlation stays negative within budget in >=2 of 3 budgets -- 3 of 3 negative

[4] PERMUTATION TEST (the n=12 correlation could be luck)
    |r| = 0.702,  195/20000 permutations at least as extreme,  p = 0.0098 (two-sided)
  [PASS] permutation test rejects the null at p<0.05 -- p=0.0098

[5] DECOMPOSITION -- the discriminating test between the two hypotheses.
    Which SIDE of (router - ink) carries the correlation?
    H_SELECT  => the ROUTER side (the router genuinely improves).
    H_COADAPT => the INK side (a fixed selection gets read worse).

    corr(router retained_ink, ROUTER recall) = +0.083
    corr(router retained_ink, INK    recall) = +0.634   <- selection CONSTANT

      keep=0.75   router-side +0.135   ink-side +0.188
      keep=0.50   router-side -0.508   ink-side +0.800
      keep=0.35   router-side -0.543   ink-side +0.372
  [PASS] the INK side carries the correlation, not the router side -- |0.634| > |0.083|

[6] PLACEBO -- a quantity that should NOT predict the margin.
    `random`'s retained_ink is ~0.50 by construction and carries no
    information about the router, so it must not correlate.
    corr(RANDOM retained_ink, margin) = -0.242   (vs the real -0.702)
  [PASS] placebo correlates more weakly than the real predictor -- |-0.242| < |-0.702|

[7] NATURAL CONTROL -- run 11 never trained under pruning.
    TRAIN_KEEP_RATIO=1.00, so its decoder was never exposed to ANY
    pruned distribution and has the least opportunity to co-adapt.
    H_COADAPT predicts its margins sit at the SMALL end.

      keep=0.75  run 11 margin  +2.10  -> rank 4 of 4 by |margin|
      keep=0.50  run 11 margin  -0.49  -> rank 1 of 4 by |margin|
      keep=0.35  run 11 margin  +0.35  -> rank 1 of 4 by |margin|

==============================================================================
VERDICT
==============================================================================
H_COADAPT is SUPPORTED and H_SELECT's prediction is CONTRADICTED.

  - The router beats ink MORE when it keeps LESS of the page's text
    (pooled r = -0.702, p = 0.0098). H_SELECT predicts the opposite sign.
  - The correlation lives on the INK side (+0.634) not the router side
    (+0.083). Ink's selection is byte-identical across checkpoints, so
    that variation is DECODER, not selection.
  - A fixed token set is read 9.54 pts differently by different
    decoders at keep=0.35, against a mean claimed margin of 2.15 pts.

  => The router-vs-ink margin substantially measures DECODER
     SPECIALIZATION, not selection quality. Claim 2 must not be
     stated against `ink` without a decoder trained for `ink`.

LIMITS (stated, not buried):
  - OBSERVATIONAL. n=12 cells from 4 checkpoints x 3 budgets.
  - The 4 checkpoints are NOT independent: all fine-tuned from run 5.
  - retained_ink is a PROXY for 'distance from ink selection'. It is the
    best one on disk, but it is a scalar summary of a set difference.
  - This CANNOT say how much of the margin is co-adaptation. Only a
    decoder trained under ink selection can (run 18).

CONTROLS: 12 passed, 0 failed
